1
00:00:04,519 --> 00:00:10,199
He lay in prison very ill, during the whole interval between his committal for trial and

2
00:00:10,199 --> 00:00:16,917
the coming round of the Sessions. He had broken two ribs, they had wounded one of his lungs,

3
00:00:16,917 --> 00:00:22,038
and he breathed with great pain and difficulty, which increased daily.

4
00:00:22,038 --> 00:00:26,519
It was a consequence of his hurt that he spoke so low as to be scarcely audible;

5
00:00:26,519 --> 00:00:32,277
therefore he spoke very little. But he was ever ready to listen to me; and it became

6
00:00:32,277 --> 00:00:39,557
the first duty of my life to say to him, and read to him, what I knew he ought to hear.

7
00:00:39,559 --> 00:00:44,718
Being far too ill to remain in the common prison, he was removed,

8
00:00:44,718 --> 00:00:51,358
after the first day or so, into the infirmary. This gave me opportunities of being with him that

9
00:00:51,358 --> 00:00:57,476
I could not otherwise have had. And but for his illness he would have been put in irons,

10
00:00:57,476 --> 00:01:03,637
for he was regarded as a determined prison-breaker, and I know not what else.

11
00:01:03,637 --> 00:01:10,680
Although I saw him every day, it was for only a short time; hence, the regularly recurring spaces

12
00:01:10,680 --> 00:01:16,758
of our separation were long enough to record on his face any slight changes that occurred in his

13
00:01:16,758 --> 00:01:23,917
physical state. I do not recollect that I once saw any change in it for the better; he wasted,

14
00:01:24,476 --> 00:01:31,358
and became slowly weaker and worse, day by day, from the day when the prison door closed upon him.

15
00:01:31,358 --> 00:01:37,917
The kind of submission or resignation that he showed was that of a man who was tired out. I

16
00:01:37,917 --> 00:01:43,319
sometimes derived an impression, from his manner or from a whispered word or two which escaped him,

17
00:01:43,319 --> 00:01:47,160
that he pondered over the question whether he might have been a better man under better

18
00:01:47,160 --> 00:01:53,277
circumstances. But he never justified himself by a hint tending that way,

19
00:01:53,277 --> 00:01:56,917
or tried to bend the past out of its eternal shape.

20
00:01:56,917 --> 00:02:01,319
It happened on two or three occasions in my presence, that his desperate reputation

21
00:02:01,319 --> 00:02:07,678
was alluded to by one or other of the people in attendance on him. A smile crossed his face then,

22
00:02:07,680 --> 00:02:12,199
and he turned his eyes on me with a trustful look, as if he were confident that I had seen

23
00:02:12,199 --> 00:02:18,398
some small redeeming touch in him, even so long ago as when I was a little child.

24
00:02:18,398 --> 00:02:23,519
As to all the rest, he was humble and contrite, and I never knew him complain.

25
00:02:23,519 --> 00:02:28,519
When the Sessions came round, Mr. Jaggers caused an application to be made for the postponement

26
00:02:28,519 --> 00:02:34,199
of his trial until the following Sessions. It was obviously made with the assurance that he

27
00:02:34,199 --> 00:02:41,957
could not live so long, and was refused. The trial came on at once, and, when he was put to the bar,

28
00:02:41,957 --> 00:02:47,398
he was seated in a chair. No objection was made to my getting close to the dock,

29
00:02:47,398 --> 00:02:53,436
on the outside of it, and holding the hand that he stretched forth to me.

30
00:02:53,438 --> 00:03:00,160
The trial was very short and very clear. Such things as could be said for him were said,—how

31
00:03:00,160 --> 00:03:06,557
he had taken to industrious habits, and had thriven lawfully and reputably. But nothing

32
00:03:06,559 --> 00:03:12,957
could unsay the fact that he had returned, and was there in presence of the Judge and Jury.

33
00:03:12,957 --> 00:03:18,358
It was impossible to try him for that, and do otherwise than find him guilty.

34
00:03:18,358 --> 00:03:23,476
At that time, it was the custom (as I learnt from my terrible experience of that Sessions)

35
00:03:23,476 --> 00:03:29,116
to devote a concluding day to the passing of Sentences, and to make a finishing effect

36
00:03:29,116 --> 00:03:35,758
with the Sentence of Death. But for the indelible picture that my remembrance now holds before me,

37
00:03:35,758 --> 00:03:41,438
I could scarcely believe, even as I write these words, that I saw two-and-thirty men

38
00:03:41,438 --> 00:03:47,917
and women put before the Judge to receive that sentence together. Foremost among

39
00:03:47,917 --> 00:03:53,718
the two-and-thirty was he; seated, that he might get breath enough to keep life in him.

40
00:03:54,879 --> 00:03:59,796
The whole scene starts out again in the vivid colours of the moment, down to the drops of April

41
00:03:59,796 --> 00:04:07,038
rain on the windows of the court, glittering in the rays of April sun. Penned in the dock, as I

42
00:04:07,038 --> 00:04:12,718
again stood outside it at the corner with his hand in mine, were the two-and-thirty men and women;

43
00:04:12,718 --> 00:04:19,277
some defiant, some stricken with terror, some sobbing and weeping, some covering their faces,

44
00:04:19,277 --> 00:04:24,678
some staring gloomily about. There had been shrieks from among the women convicts; but

45
00:04:24,680 --> 00:04:32,160
they had been stilled, and a hush had succeeded. The sheriffs with their great chains and nosegays,

46
00:04:32,160 --> 00:04:40,116
other civic gewgaws and monsters, criers, ushers, a great gallery full of people,—a large theatrical

47
00:04:40,116 --> 00:04:45,917
audience,—looked on, as the two-and-thirty and the Judge were solemnly confronted. Then

48
00:04:45,917 --> 00:04:51,718
the Judge addressed them. Among the wretched creatures before him whom he must single out

49
00:04:51,718 --> 00:04:59,078
for special address was one who almost from his infancy had been an offender against the laws;

50
00:04:59,078 --> 00:05:03,957
who, after repeated imprisonments and punishments, had been at length sentenced

51
00:05:03,957 --> 00:05:10,597
to exile for a term of years; and who, under circumstances of great violence and daring,

52
00:05:10,597 --> 00:05:17,519
had made his escape and been re-sentenced to exile for life. That miserable man would seem for a

53
00:05:17,519 --> 00:05:23,957
time to have become convinced of his errors, when far removed from the scenes of his old offences,

54
00:05:23,957 --> 00:05:30,839
and to have lived a peaceable and honest life. But in a fatal moment, yielding to those propensities

55
00:05:30,839 --> 00:05:37,438
and passions, the indulgence of which had so long rendered him a scourge to society, he had

56
00:05:37,438 --> 00:05:44,000
quitted his haven of rest and repentance, and had come back to the country where he was proscribed.

57
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:49,839
Being here presently denounced, he had for a time succeeded in evading the officers of Justice,

58
00:05:49,839 --> 00:05:55,597
but being at length seized while in the act of flight, he had resisted them, and had—he best knew

59
00:05:55,597 --> 00:06:02,277
whether by express design, or in the blindness of his hardihood—caused the death of his denouncer,

60
00:06:02,277 --> 00:06:08,116
to whom his whole career was known. The appointed punishment for his return to the land that had

61
00:06:08,116 --> 00:06:16,597
cast him out, being Death, and his case being this aggravated case, he must prepare himself to Die.

62
00:06:16,597 --> 00:06:21,358
The sun was striking in at the great windows of the court, through the glittering drops of rain

63
00:06:21,358 --> 00:06:26,838
upon the glass, and it made a broad shaft of light between the two-and-thirty and the Judge,

64
00:06:26,839 --> 00:06:31,917
linking both together, and perhaps reminding some among the audience how both were passing on,

65
00:06:31,917 --> 00:06:39,000
with absolute equality, to the greater Judgment that knoweth all things, and cannot err. Rising

66
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:44,559
for a moment, a distinct speck of face in this way of light, the prisoner said,

67
00:06:44,559 --> 00:06:50,199
“My Lord, I have received my sentence of Death from the Almighty, but I bow to yours,” and

68
00:06:50,199 --> 00:06:56,000
sat down again. There was some hushing, and the Judge went on with what he had to

69
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:02,358
say to the rest. Then they were all formally doomed, and some of them were supported out,

70
00:07:02,358 --> 00:07:08,398
and some of them sauntered out with a haggard look of bravery, and a few nodded to the gallery,

71
00:07:08,398 --> 00:07:14,160
and two or three shook hands, and others went out chewing the fragments of herb they had taken from

72
00:07:14,160 --> 00:07:20,796
the sweet herbs lying about. He went last of all, because of having to be helped from his chair,

73
00:07:20,796 --> 00:07:25,519
and to go very slowly; and he held my hand while all the others were removed,

74
00:07:25,519 --> 00:07:29,957
and while the audience got up (putting their dresses right, as they might at church or

75
00:07:29,957 --> 00:07:37,476
elsewhere), and pointed down at this criminal or at that, and most of all at him and me.

76
00:07:37,476 --> 00:07:42,917
I earnestly hoped and prayed that he might die before the Recorder’s Report was made; but, in

77
00:07:42,917 --> 00:07:47,758
the dread of his lingering on, I began that night to write out a petition to the Home Secretary of

78
00:07:47,758 --> 00:07:53,438
State, setting forth my knowledge of him, and how it was that he had come back for my sake.

79
00:07:54,038 --> 00:07:59,839
I wrote it as fervently and pathetically as I could; and when I had finished it and sent it in,

80
00:07:59,839 --> 00:08:04,957
I wrote out other petitions to such men in authority as I hoped were the most merciful,

81
00:08:04,957 --> 00:08:11,436
and drew up one to the Crown itself. For several days and nights after he was sentenced I took no

82
00:08:11,438 --> 00:08:17,559
rest except when I fell asleep in my chair, but was wholly absorbed in these appeals.

83
00:08:17,559 --> 00:08:21,957
And after I had sent them in, I could not keep away from the places where they were,

84
00:08:21,957 --> 00:08:27,000
but felt as if they were more hopeful and less desperate when I was near them. In

85
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:31,957
this unreasonable restlessness and pain of mind I would roam the streets of an evening,

86
00:08:31,957 --> 00:08:38,758
wandering by those offices and houses where I had left the petitions. To the present hour,

87
00:08:38,758 --> 00:08:44,597
the weary western streets of London on a cold, dusty spring night, with their ranges of stern,

88
00:08:44,597 --> 00:08:52,319
shut-up mansions, and their long rows of lamps, are melancholy to me from this association.

89
00:08:52,319 --> 00:08:58,277
The daily visits I could make him were shortened now, and he was more strictly kept. Seeing,

90
00:08:58,277 --> 00:09:03,879
or fancying, that I was suspected of an intention of carrying poison to him, I asked to be searched

91
00:09:03,879 --> 00:09:09,637
before I sat down at his bedside, and told the officer who was always there, that I was willing

92
00:09:09,637 --> 00:09:15,597
to do anything that would assure him of the singleness of my designs. Nobody was hard with

93
00:09:15,597 --> 00:09:23,839
him or with me. There was duty to be done, and it was done, but not harshly. The officer always

94
00:09:23,839 --> 00:09:28,758
gave me the assurance that he was worse, and some other sick prisoners in the room, and some other

95
00:09:28,758 --> 00:09:34,199
prisoners who attended on them as sick nurses, (malefactors, but not incapable of kindness,

96
00:09:34,199 --> 00:09:41,398
God be thanked!) always joined in the same report. As the days went on, I noticed more and more that

97
00:09:41,398 --> 00:09:46,718
he would lie placidly looking at the white ceiling, with an absence of light in his

98
00:09:46,718 --> 00:09:53,321
face until some word of mine brightened it for an instant, and then it would subside again.

99
00:09:54,319 --> 00:09:59,000
Sometimes he was almost or quite unable to speak, then he would answer me with

100
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:04,718
slight pressures on my hand, and I grew to understand his meaning very well.

101
00:10:04,718 --> 00:10:08,597
The number of the days had risen to ten, when I saw a greater change in

102
00:10:08,597 --> 00:10:16,000
him than I had seen yet. His eyes were turned towards the door, and lighted up as I entered.

103
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:20,000
“Dear boy,” he said, as I sat down by his bed:

104
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:23,319
“I thought you was late. But I knowed you couldn’t be that.”

105
00:10:24,879 --> 00:10:29,277
“It is just the time,” said I. “I waited for it at the gate.”

106
00:10:29,277 --> 00:10:32,116
“You always waits at the gate; don’t you, dear boy?”

107
00:10:32,116 --> 00:10:35,237
“Yes. Not to lose a moment of the time.”

108
00:10:35,237 --> 00:10:40,957
“Thank’ee dear boy, thank’ee. God bless you! You’ve never deserted me, dear boy.”

109
00:10:40,957 --> 00:10:48,000
I pressed his hand in silence, for I could not forget that I had once meant to desert him.

110
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:52,237
“And what’s the best of all,” he said, “you’ve been more comfortable alonger me,

111
00:10:52,237 --> 00:10:57,917
since I was under a dark cloud, than when the sun shone. That’s best of all.”

112
00:10:57,917 --> 00:11:03,160
He lay on his back, breathing with great difficulty. Do what he would,

113
00:11:03,160 --> 00:11:07,557
and love me though he did, the light left his face ever and again,

114
00:11:07,559 --> 00:11:11,796
and a film came over the placid look at the white ceiling.

115
00:11:11,796 --> 00:11:14,116
“Are you in much pain to-day?”

116
00:11:14,116 --> 00:11:16,519
“I don’t complain of none, dear boy.”

117
00:11:16,519 --> 00:11:19,438
“You never do complain.”

118
00:11:19,438 --> 00:11:23,277
He had spoken his last words. He smiled,

119
00:11:23,277 --> 00:11:26,756
and I understood his touch to mean that he wished to lift my hand,

120
00:11:26,758 --> 00:11:35,637
and lay it on his breast. I laid it there, and he smiled again, and put both his hands upon it.

121
00:11:35,637 --> 00:11:40,237
The allotted time ran out, while we were thus; but, looking round,

122
00:11:40,237 --> 00:11:45,116
I found the governor of the prison standing near me, and he whispered, “You needn’t go

123
00:11:45,116 --> 00:11:51,796
yet.” I thanked him gratefully, and asked, “Might I speak to him, if he can hear me?”

124
00:11:51,796 --> 00:11:56,597
The governor stepped aside, and beckoned the officer away. The change,

125
00:11:56,597 --> 00:12:00,038
though it was made without noise, drew back the film from the placid

126
00:12:00,038 --> 00:12:05,116
look at the white ceiling, and he looked most affectionately at me.

127
00:12:05,116 --> 00:12:12,116
“Dear Magwitch, I must tell you now, at last. You understand what I say?”

128
00:12:12,116 --> 00:12:14,718
A gentle pressure on my hand.

129
00:12:14,718 --> 00:12:18,957
“You had a child once, whom you loved and lost.”

130
00:12:18,957 --> 00:12:21,519
A stronger pressure on my hand.

131
00:12:21,519 --> 00:12:25,000
“She lived, and found powerful friends. She

132
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:31,319
is living now. She is a lady and very beautiful. And I love her!”

133
00:12:31,319 --> 00:12:36,597
With a last faint effort, which would have been powerless but for my yielding to it and assisting

134
00:12:36,597 --> 00:12:43,879
it, he raised my hand to his lips. Then, he gently let it sink upon his breast again,

135
00:12:43,879 --> 00:12:49,879
with his own hands lying on it. The placid look at the white ceiling came back,

136
00:12:49,879 --> 00:12:54,917
and passed away, and his head dropped quietly on his breast.

137
00:12:54,917 --> 00:13:00,438
Mindful, then, of what we had read together, I thought of the two men who went up into the Temple

138
00:13:00,438 --> 00:13:07,398
to pray, and I knew there were no better words that I could say beside his bed, than “O Lord,

139
00:13:07,398 --> 00:13:10,000
be merciful to him a sinner!” Chapter 57.
